directive:
D-orchestrator directive for angle 0 revision v3. The v2 review cleared every v1 defect (constant-overloading, BKW title, matched-assumption table) and again judged BOTH theorems NEW on both axes at tier=field. The kernel is accepted: do NOT change the research question, the two theorem targets, or the OEQ. Two classes of defect remain. Fix each WHOLESALE — do not patch one instance and resubmit, because a one-at-a-time patch is what makes these rotate.

(A) ONE EXHAUSTIVE WELL-POSEDNESS AUDIT ACROSS ALL NODES (soundness flag C-wellposed).
Do a single pass over def:holder-class, def:estimator-class, def:minimax-mse, def:oracle-rate, thm:beta-invariance, thm:oracle-high-s and oeq:deficient-frontier, and close EVERY definedness hole at once, not just the two named below.

  (A1) Measurable-version selection. mu_P(a,x) = E_P[Y | A=a, X=x] and pi_P(a|x) are determined only up to P-null sets, so a pointwise "for every x" Hoelder requirement is not a property of P as currently written. Define class membership through EXISTENCE of designated versions: P belongs to the class iff there EXIST a jointly Borel-measurable mu: [0,1] x [0,1]^d -> [-M_Y, M_Y] and a jointly Borel-measurable conditional density pi: [0,1] x [0,1]^d -> R_{>=0} with respect to Lebesgue measure on [0,1], such that mu is a version of the outcome regression under P, pi is a version of the conditional treatment density under P, and the four Hoelder conditions and the positivity bounds hold for those designated versions at EVERY (a,x) in the full cube. State that theta_P(t_0) = int mu(t_0,x) p_X(x) dx is then well defined and, because any two admissible designated versions agree Lebesgue-a.e. in x and both are continuous in x, does not depend on the choice of admissible version. Also fix the version convention for p_X (a designated Borel density with respect to Lebesgue on [0,1]^d, bounded above by pbar).

  (A2) The beta = infinity convention. Theorem 1 quantifies over beta_prime <= infinity, but "Hoelder of order infinity with radius L" is undefined. Choose ONE convention and state it explicitly in def:holder-class, then use it consistently in every statement: either (i) restrict beta and beta_prime to finite values in (0, infinity) and delete the infinity endpoint from thm:beta-invariance, or (ii) define the beta = infinity class explicitly as the laws whose designated pi(., x) is infinitely differentiable in a with all derivatives bounded by L uniformly in x. Option (i) is the safer default and costs nothing scientifically -- the beta-invariance content is already carried by all finite pairs 0 < beta < beta_prime < infinity -- so take (i) unless you have a reason to need the endpoint. If you take (ii), state the derivative bound convention precisely.

  (A3) Sweep for any remaining definedness gaps in the SAME pass, and state each resolution explicitly: that t_0 lies in the interior of [0,1] and, if the argument needs a two-sided neighbourhood of t_0 inside [0,1], say so in the domain declaration; that the Hoelder exponents alpha, beta, s use a stated convention for exponents greater than 1 (floor-derivative plus remainder-exponent form) or are restricted to (0,1] if the proposal does not need larger; that the anisotropic conditions are stated coordinatewise in the sense intended for x in dimension d; and that def:minimax-mse's supremum is over a class already certified nonempty by the witness law (keep the v2 nonemptiness statement and check the witness still satisfies the version-based definition and the chosen infinity convention).

(B) ONE COMPLETE COMPARATOR SET, WRITTEN IN FULL THIS ROUND (novelty flags N-mischar and N-thin-survey).
The v2 review removed one comparator defect and surfaced two more. Stop adding comparators one at a time. Rewrite related_work so that it covers EVERY distinct close prior-art APPROACH FAMILY to this question, each with an explicit differentiation on the same set of axes, so no further single-comparator request is possible. Use at minimum these families, and add any other you identify:
  (i) kernel-smoothed / doubly robust pointwise continuous-treatment estimation -- KennedyMaMcHughSmall2017, and ColangeloLee (double debiased machine learning with continuous treatments);
  (ii) higher-order influence function and nonlinear functional minimax estimation -- RobinsLiTchetgenVanderVaart2008HOIF, LiuMukherjeeRobins2021AdaptiveFunctionals, BonviniKennedy2022FastDRF;
  (iii) minimax elbow results for binary-treatment CATE -- KennedyBalakrishnanRobinsWasserman2024MinimaxCATE;
  (iv) structure-agnostic functional-estimation limits -- BalakrishnanKennedyWasserman2023;
  (v) incremental / tilted continuous-exposure targets -- SchindlShenKennedy;
  (vi) integrated-risk RKHS / Sobolev lower bounds for dose-response -- the Kim-Wang line cited in the anchor;
  (vii) the in-repo banked run stat_dose_response_minimax / holder_anisotropic_converse.
For every family use the SAME four differentiation axes, stated explicitly: (a) the target functional (fixed interior dose partial mean vs curve-wide, incremental, integrated, or binary-CATE target); (b) the risk notion (unconditional minimax MSE over a declared law class vs conditional-on-nuisance risk vs an upper bound for one specified estimator); (c) whether a treatment-density smoothness index appears in the class at all; (d) what the result would imply, or fail to imply, about beta-invariance in exactly this class. The point of each entry is why it does NOT resolve the proposed question -- never that it is a substitute.

  (B1) Correct the Schindl entry. My previous directive told you to characterize that work as an edge-of-support / non-localizing target; the reviewer verified that this is INACCURATE -- the current arXiv version builds a reflected exponential tilt around an arbitrary interior point and takes a limit to recover the dose-response curve across the support. Discard my earlier characterization entirely. Go to the actual arXiv source (use the HTML / LaTeX rendering, not the PDF), read how the target is defined, and write a differentiation that is true of what the paper actually does -- distinguish on the verified axes above (target functional and the presence or absence of a treatment-density smoothness coordinate in the assumed class), and cite a specific locator for what you assert. Do not assert any characterization of any paper you have not verified against its source this round; if you cannot verify a claim, state the weaker verified fact instead.

(C) WHAT NOT TO DO. The review's third strengthening path asks for a full theorem-side specification of the beta-free estimator (localized moment, normalization, projection family, tuning rule). Do NOT do this: it is a strengthening path, not a flag, and the estimator and every mechanic inside it are deliberately NOT pre-committed in this run -- constructing them is the Stage 0 derivation work. Keep def:beta-free-handle as a declared derivation handle, and add one sentence to the iteration trail recording this decision and its reason, so it is not re-requested. Equally, do NOT add any assumption that encodes the crux (no assumed beta-freeness, no assumed negligible HOIF or density-estimation remainder, no extra covariate-density smoothness) to make the targets easier.

Keep tldr, project_justification, related_work and comparator_promise_table in step with every wording change above.
